<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch10-section-barotropy-baroclinity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Barotropy and baroclinity</title>

  <p>
    A <term>barotropic</term> fluid is one in which density <m>\rho</m> depends only on pressure <m>p</m>,
    i.e.,
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-barotropic-density" number="yes">
        \rho = \rho(p)
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Consider this restriction alongside the ideal gas law:
    Since <m>\rho</m> depends only on <m>p</m>,
    the ideal gas law implies that temperature <m>T</m> also depends only on <m>p</m>,
    i.e.,
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-barotropic-temperature" number="yes">
        T = T(p)
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Consequently, isobaric, <term>isopycnic</term> (constant density), and isothermal surfaces
    are parallel (i.e., stacked vertically) within a barotropic fluid,
    as shown in <xref ref="ch10-barotropic" text="type-global" /> below.
    In other words,
    isobaric surfaces also are ispycnic and isothermal surfaces.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="ch10-barotropic">
    <caption>
        Stratification of isobaric (black), isopycnic (aqua), and isothermal (dashed red) surfaces
        within a barotropic fluid.
        The surfaces are parallel and tilt upward where the fluid is warmer.
        Figure and caption modified from image vectorised by User:Sushant savla from the work by Reas at German Wikipedia,
        via <url href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barotrope.svg">Wikimedia Commons</url>
        under <url href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0">CC BY-SA 4.0</url>.
    </caption>
    <image source="images/ch10-barotropic.png" width="80%">
        <shortdescription>
            Rectangular prism volume of barotropic fluid with parallel black, aqua, and dashed red surfaces
            representing isobaric, isopycnic, and isothermal surfaces, respectively.
            The surfaces tilt upward where the fluid is warmer.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    It follows that in a barotropic atmosphere,
    <m>T</m> does not vary along isobaric surfaces,
    i.e., <m>\nabla_p T = \vec{0}</m>.
    Consequently,
    <xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-balance-vector" text="type-global" /><ndash /><xref ref="eqn-thermal-wind-balance-y" text="type-global" /> require that
    the geostrophic wind is not vertically sheared in a barotropic atmosphere,
    i.e., it is constant with height so <m>-\dfrac{\partial \vec{v}_g}{\partial p} = \vec{0}</m>.
    This means the thermal wind also is zero in a barotropic atmosphere: <m>\vec{v}_T = \vec{0}</m>.
    So in a barotropic atmosphere,
    the geostrophic wind can vary with horizontal position and time but not with height.
    In other words,
    at a given moment in time,
    the geostrophic wind is constant in the vertical over any location of interest within a barotropic atmosphere.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <term>baroclinic</term> fluid is one in which <m>\rho</m> depends on both <m>p</m> and <m>T</m>,
    i.e.,
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-baroclinic-density" number="yes">
        \rho = \rho(p,T)
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    Consider this statement alongside the ideal gas law:
    Since <m>\rho</m> depends on both <m>p</m> and <m>T</m>,
    the ideal gas law implies that <m>T</m> depends on both <m>p</m> and <m>\rho</m>,
    i.e.,
  </p>

  <p>
    <md xml:id="eqn-baroclinic-temperature" number="yes">
        T = T(p,\rho)
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    This is a more complicated situation than for the barotropic fluid,
    and it means isobaric, isopycnic, and isothermal surfaces are not parallel in a baroclinic fluid,
    an example of which is shown in <xref ref="ch10-baroclinic" text="type-global" /> below.
    Consequently,
    in a baroclinic atmosphere,
    temperature can vary along isobaric surfaces,
    and so there is no restriction on the vertical shear of the geostrophic wind and the thermal wind:
    The geostrophic wind can vary with horizontal position and time as well as with height
    as long as temperature gradients are present along isobaric surfaces.
    Furthermore, the thermal wind will not be zero.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="ch10-baroclinic">
    <caption>
        Possible stratification of isobaric (black), isopycnic (aqua), and isothermal (dashed red) surfaces
        within a baroclinic fluid.
        The surfaces are not parallel,
        and the isopycnic surfaces tilt downward where the fluid is warmer,
        indicating lower density in the warmer part of the fluid.
        Figure and caption modified from image vectorised by User:Sushant savla from the work by Reas at German Wikipedia,
        via <url href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baroclinic_fluid.png">Wikimedia Commons</url>
        under <url href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/">CC BY-SA 3.0</url>.
    </caption>
    <image source="images/ch10-baroclinic.png" width="80%">
        <shortdescription>
            Rectangular prism volume of barotropic fluid with parallel black, aqua, and dashed red surfaces
            representing isobaric, isopycnic, and isothermal surfaces, respectively.
            The surfaces are not parallel,
            and the isopycnic surfaces tilt downward where the fluid is warmer.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    We can determine where the atmosphere is barotropic,
    approximately barotropic,
    or baroclinic using isobaric maps with isotherms contoured on them.
  </p>

  <p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Where the atmosphere is barotropic,
                isotherms will not be drawn on the map since temperature is constant on the isobaric surface.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Where the atmosphere is approximately barotropic,
                few isotherms will be drawn on the map,
                and they will be spaced relatively far apart from each other.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Where the atmosphere is baroclinic,
                many isotherms will be drawn on the map since temperature varies across the isobaric surface,
                and they will be spaced relatively close to each other.
                An example of this case is shown in <xref ref="ch10-baroclinic-map" text="type-global" /> below.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    Several isobaric maps for different pressure levels can be compared to determine the depth over which the atmosphere is barotropic,
    approximately barotropic,
    or baroclinic.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="ch10-baroclinic-map">
    <caption>
        850 hPa map with contoured isohypses of geopotential height (solid black; m),
        isotherms (dashed red for temperatures above 0°C, dashed blue for temperatures at and below 0°C),
        and isodrosotherms (dotted green for dewpoints at or above 0°C, shaded green for dewpoints at or above 6°C).
        Station models and pressure centers are annotated.
        Map courtesy of <url href="https://weather.cod.edu/">College of DuPage Weather Lab</url>.
    </caption>
    <image source="images/ch10-baroclinic-map.png" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
            850 hPa map with many closely spaced isotherms,
            indicating baroclinity of the atmosphere at this pressure level.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    In general,
    baroclinity is maximized in the middle latitudes,
    while the tropics are approximately barotropic.
    <xref ref="ch10-500mb-temp" text="type-global" /> below
    shows 1991<ndash />2020 annual average isotherms on the 500 hPa isobaric surface.
    In <xref ref="ch10-500mb-temp" text="type-global" />,
    the horizontal temperature gradient is quite small in the tropics as depicted by the distant spacing of a couple of isotherms,
    indicating approximate barotropy.
    In contrast,
    the close spacing of many isotherms in the mid-latitudes indicates baroclinity.
    Baroclinity persists but decreases in the polar latitudes as indicated by the relatively more distant spacing of fewer isotherms.
    Similar patterns are apparent on isobaric maps for other pressure levels below the tropopause (not shown).
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="ch10-500mb-temp">
    <caption>
        1991<ndash />2020 annual average isotherms on the 500 hPa isobaric surface.
        Isotherms are contoured at 5°C increments and labeled every 10°C,
        with shading between isotherms giving temperature according to the color bar.
        Warmer colored shading indicates warmer temperature.
        Figure courtesy of <url href="https://psl.noaa.gov/">NOAA Physical Sciences Laboratory</url>
        using NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis data.
    </caption>
    <image source="images/ch10-500mb-temp.png" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
            Global 500 mb map with nearly uniform warm air in the tropics,
            highly baroclinic air in the middle latitudes that cools with increasing latitude,
            and somewhat baroclinic air in the polar latitudes that cools with increasing latitude.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    <xref ref="ch10-zonal-temp-annual" text="type-global" /> below
    shows a vertical cross-section of 1991<ndash />2020 annual average isotherms that also have been averaged longitudinally.
    As expected,
    the isotherms depict the warmest air on average in the tropics and the coldest air on average in the polar regions,
    through the depth of the troposphere,
    thus setting up a meridional temperature gradient in both the Northern Hemisphere and the Southern Hemisphere.
    Furthermore,
    the isotherms approximately parallel isobaric surfaces in the tropics but are steeply sloped in the middle latitudes
    and intersect isobaric surfaces up to about 200 hPa.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="ch10-zonal-temp-annual">
    <caption>
        1991<ndash />2020 annual average isotherms also averaged longitudinally.
        Isotherms are contoured at 5°C increments and labeled every 10°C,
        with shading between isotherms giving temperature according to the color bar.
        Warmer colored shading indicates warmer temperature.
        Isotherms colder than 0°C are dashed
        while isotherms equal to and warmer than 0°C are solid.
        Pressure (hPa) is shown on the vertical axis and latitude is shown on the horizontal axis.
        Figure courtesy of <url href="https://psl.noaa.gov/">NOAA Physical Sciences Laboratory</url>
        using NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis data.
    </caption>
    <image source="images/ch10-zonal-temp-annual.png" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
            Zonally averaged temperature at all latitudes from 1000 hPa to 1 hPa.
            A deep layer of warm air is found in the tropics up to about 250 hPa.
            The warm air slopes downward with increasing latitude in the middle latitudes and polar regions,
            creating relatively colder air aloft.
            The coldest air in the stratosphere is found above the tropics.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Given our previous discussion,
    it should be unsurprising that zonal winds on average are maximized in the middle latitudes as <term>jet streams</term>
    (narrow, horizontally-oriented fast-moving currents of air),
    with wind speed maxima on average around 200 hPa
    (<xref ref="ch10-zonal-wind-annual" text="type-global" />).
    This approximately is the pressure level above which the meridional temperature gradient reverses direction,
    with the coldest air in the stratosphere found above the tropics.
    Average zonal winds at other latitudes where baroclinity is smaller are weaker and closer to constant with height.
    Furthermore,
    average zonal winds in and around the jet streams are westerly,
    consistent with the westerly vertical wind shear of the geostrophic wind required by thermal wind balance
    in both the Northern and Southern Hemispheres.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="ch10-zonal-wind-annual">
    <caption>
        1991<ndash />2020 annual average isotachs of zonal wind also averaged longitudinally.
        Isotachs are contoured at <m>5 \, \text{m} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m> increments
        and labeled every <m>10 \, \text{m} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m>,
        with shading between isotachs giving wind speedaccording to the color bar.
        Isotachs of westerly winds (directed out of the screen) are solid,
        and isotachs of easterly winds (directed into the screen) are dashed.
        Pressure (hPa) is shown on the vertical axis and latitude is shown on the horizontal axis.
        Figure courtesy of <url href="https://psl.noaa.gov/">NOAA Physical Sciences Laboratory</url>
        using NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis data.
    </caption>
    <image source="images/ch10-zonal-wind-annual.png" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>
            Zonally averaged zonal wind at all latitudes from 1000 hPa to 1 hPa.
            The largest vertical wind shear and fastest winds aloft are found over the middle latitudes,
            with speed maxima around 200 hPa.
        </shortdescription>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Baroclinity is enhanced in the winter hemisphere relative to the summer hemisphere
    because the meridional temperature gradient is strengthened as the polar regions cool.
    <xref ref="ch10-zonal-temp-djf" text="type-global" /> below
    shows a vertical cross-section of 1991<ndash />2020 isotherms that have been averaged over December-January-February (DFJ)
    as well as longitudinally,
    and <xref ref="ch10-zonal-temp-jja" text="type-global" /> below
    shows a vertical cross-section of 1991<ndash />2020 isotherms that have been averaged over June-July-August (JJA)
    as well as longitudinally.
    The former three-month period is winter (summer) for the Northern (Southern) Hemisphere,
    and the latter three-month period is winter (summer) for the Southern (Northern) Hemisphere.
    As in the annual average shown in <xref ref="ch10-zonal-temp-annual" text="type-global" />,
    the isotherms approximately parallel isobaric surfaces in the tropics
    but are steeply sloped in the middle latitudes and intersect isobaric surfaces up to about 200 hPa.
    Furthermore,
    isotherms are sloped more steeply in the middle latitudes of the winter hemisphere relative to the summer hemisphere
    and intersect isobaric surfaces more frequently,
    indicating enhanced baroclinity.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unsurprisingly,
    seasonally-averaged zonal winds indicate jet streams are faster in the middle latitudes of the winter hemisphere,
    as shown in <xref ref="ch10-zonal-wind-seasonal" text="type-global" /> below.
    Again,
    wind speed maxima are found around 200 hPa,
    above which the meridional temperature gradient reverses direction in the stratosphere.
  </p>

    <figure xml:id="ch10-zonal-temp-seasonal">
        <caption />
        <sidebyside>
            <figure xml:id="ch10-zonal-temp-djf">
                <caption>
                    1991<ndash />2020 isotherms averaged over December-January-February (DFJ)
                    as well as longitudinally.
                    Isotherms are contoured at 5°C increments and labeled every 10°C,
                    with color shading between isotherms according to the color bar.
                    Warmer colored shading indicates warmer temperature.
                    Isotherms colder than 0°C are dashed while isotherms equal to and warmer than 0°C are solid.
                    Pressure (hPa) is shown on the vertical axis and latitude is shown on the horizontal axis.
                    Figure courtesy of <url href="https://psl.noaa.gov/">NOAA Physical Sciences Laboratory</url>
                    using NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis data.
                </caption>
                <image source="images/ch10-zonal-temp-djf.png" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>
                      Zonally averaged temperature at all latitudes from 1000 hPa to 1 hPa.
                      A deep layer of warm air is found in the tropics up to about 250 hPa.
                      The warm air slopes downward with increasing latitude in the middle latitudes and polar regions,
                      creating relatively colder air aloft.
                      This slope is steeper in the winter hemisphere.
                      The coldest air in the stratosphere is found above the tropics.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="ch10-zonal-temp-jja">
                <caption>
                    1991<ndash />2020 isotherms averaged over June-July-August (JJA)
                    as well as longitudinally.
                    Isotherms are contoured at 5°C increments and labeled every 10°C,
                    with color shading between isotherms according to the color bar.
                    Warmer colored shading indicates warmer temperature.
                    Isotherms colder than 0°C are dashed while isotherms equal to and warmer than 0°C are solid.
                    Pressure (hPa) is shown on the vertical axis and latitude is shown on the horizontal axis.
                    Figure courtesy of <url href="https://psl.noaa.gov/">NOAA Physical Sciences Laboratory</url>
                    using NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis data.
                </caption>
                <image source="images/ch10-zonal-temp-jja.png" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>
                      Zonally averaged temperature at all latitudes from 1000 hPa to 1 hPa.
                      A deep layer of warm air is found in the tropics up to about 250 hPa.
                      The warm air slopes downward with increasing latitude in the middle latitudes and polar regions,
                      creating relatively colder air aloft.
                      This slope is steeper in the winter hemisphere.
                      The coldest air in the stratosphere is found above the tropics.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </sidebyside>
    </figure>

<figure xml:id="ch10-zonal-wind-seasonal">
        <caption />
        <sidebyside>
            <figure xml:id="ch10-zonal-wind-djf">
                <caption>
                    1991<ndash />2020 isotachs of zonal wind averaged over December-January-February (DFJ)
                    as well as longitudinally.
                    Isotachs are contoured at <m>5 \, \text{m} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m> increments
                    and labeled every <m>10 \, \text{m} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m>,
                    with color shading between isotachs according to the color bar.
                    Isotachs of westerly winds (directed out of the screen) are solid,
                    and isotachs of easterly winds (directed into the screen) are dashed.
                    Pressure (hPa) is shown on the vertical axis and latitude is shown on the horizontal axis.
                    Figure courtesy of <url href="https://psl.noaa.gov/">NOAA Physical Sciences Laboratory</url>
                    using NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis data.
                </caption>
                <image source="images/ch10-zonal-wind-djf.png" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>
                      Zonally averaged zonal wind at all latitudes from 1000 hPa to 1 hPa.
                      The largest vertical wind shear and fastest winds aloft are found over the middle latitudes,
                      with speed maxima around 200 hPa.
                      Winds are fastest in the winter hemisphere.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="ch10-zonal-wind-jja">
                <caption>
                    1991<ndash />2020 isotachs of zonal wind averaged over June-July-August (JJA)
                    as well as longitudinally.
                    Isotachs are contoured at <m>5 \, \text{m} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m> increments
                    and labeled every <m>10 \, \text{m} \, \text{s}^{-1}</m>,
                    with color shading between isotachs according to the color bar.
                    Isotachs of westerly winds (directed out of the screen) are solid,
                    and isotachs of easterly winds (directed into the screen) are dashed.
                    Pressure (hPa) is shown on the vertical axis and latitude is shown on the horizontal axis.
                    Figure courtesy of <url href="https://psl.noaa.gov/">NOAA Physical Sciences Laboratory</url>
                    using NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis data.
                </caption>
                <image source="images/ch10-zonal-wind-jja.png" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>
                      Zonally averaged zonal wind at all latitudes from 1000 hPa to 1 hPa.
                      The largest vertical wind shear and fastest winds aloft are found over the middle latitudes,
                      with speed maxima around 200 hPa.
                      Winds are fastest in the winter hemisphere.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </sidebyside>
    </figure>

</section>